        <div id="how-damage-happens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Does Ice Melt Damage Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mostly indirectly: it turns ice into water that soaks into the slab, refreezes and flakes the surface away. Some chemicals add a direct chemical attack on top of that, and the quality of the concrete decides how much either one matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does freeze-thaw cause scaling?</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete is porous. As ice melts on the surface, water and brine seep into the top layer of the slab. When the temperature falls again, that water freezes and expands by about 9 percent, pressing on the surrounding cement paste. Repeat the cycle dozens of times in a winter and the top layer starts to flake and pop off, a problem called scaling. De-icers raise the number of freeze-thaw cycles the surface goes through.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does salt add stress of its own?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. Brine absorbed into concrete forms layers with different salt concentrations, which freeze at different temperatures and create uneven pressure inside the slab. Salt crystals growing in the pores as brine dries can add pressure as well. That is why scaling tends to be worst along the edges of driveways and walks, where brine gathers and sits, and in low spots where meltwater pools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which chemicals attack the cement paste directly?</h3>

          <p>
            Research by transportation agencies and universities has tied calcium chloride and magnesium chloride to chemical reactions with the cement paste, including the formation of calcium oxychloride, an expansive compound that can damage concrete even without freeze-thaw cycles. Magnesium can also break down the binding compounds in the paste. Rock salt is less chemically aggressive than those two, but it still drives freeze-thaw scaling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why do some slabs hold up better than others?</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete quality decides how much any of this matters. Properly air-entrained concrete, finished correctly and cured for weeks, contains millions of tiny air bubbles that give freezing water room to expand, and it tolerates de-icers far better than a slab that was overwatered, overworked during finishing or poured without air entrainment. Many residential slabs fall into that weaker group, which is why choosing a gentler product is worth the effort.
          </p>

