        <div id="avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Ice Melt Should You Never Use on Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Never use ammonium-based fertilizer as a de-icer, and keep straight calcium or magnesium chloride off new, decorative or already scaling concrete. Homemade mixes and vague safety claims deserve caution too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is fertilizer off-limits?</h3>

          <p>
            Ammonium nitrate and ammonium sulfate do melt ice, and people sometimes grab them from the garage in a pinch, but ammonium compounds chemically attack concrete and can cause severe deterioration quickly. Cement and concrete industry guidance warns against them specifically. If a bag of fertilizer is all you have, spread sand for traction instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When are straight chlorides too risky?</h3>

          <p>
            Reserve straight calcium chloride and magnesium chloride for older, well-built concrete in good condition. Keep them off new slabs, stamped or stained decorative concrete and concrete that is already scaling, where any added stress speeds up the damage. When you do use them, put down small amounts and shovel the brine away quickly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do homemade de-icer mixes work?</h3>

          <p>
            Only in tiny doses. Mixtures of dish soap, rubbing alcohol and hot water can handle a step or two, but pouring hot water on frozen concrete causes thermal shock and more freeze-thaw cycling, and the runoff refreezes into a thin glaze. Treat them as a stopgap, not a winter strategy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you trust concrete-safe claims on the label?</h3>

          <p>
            Only with caution. Phrases such as safe for concrete or won't harm concrete are marketing, and they usually mean less harmful than rock salt under normal use. Look for the ingredient list, the stated working temperature and any fine print about new concrete; many reputable brands warn against use on concrete under a year old even while they advertise concrete safety.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-concrete" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can You Use Ice Melt on New Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not during its first winter. Concrete poured within the past year should get shoveling and sand, not de-icing chemicals, and some contractors extend that to two winters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is new concrete so vulnerable?</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete under a year old is still gaining strength, holds more internal moisture and has not yet built up resistance at the surface. Concrete industry guidance commonly says to use no de-icing chemicals during the first winter, and some contractors suggest avoiding them for the first two winters, especially when the slab was poured late in the season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you use on new concrete instead?</h3>

          <p>
            Shovel and add traction. Clear snow promptly, before it packs down. On ice, spread clean sand, chicken grit or a non-clumping traction material for grip. Sand melts nothing, but it keeps people on their feet without putting chemicals into a young slab. Sweep it up in spring so it is not tracked indoors or washed into drains.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does hidden salt come from?</h3>

          <p>
            Cars carry brine from city streets and drip it onto a new driveway all winter. Rinse the slab during thaws when temperatures allow, and lay a garage mat if the new concrete is inside the garage. Neighbors and snow-removal services may salt your walk without thinking about it, so ask them not to.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should you seal new concrete before winter?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, once it has cured long enough. New concrete needs to cure, typically at least 28 days, before a penetrating sealer can go on, and sealer makers set their own minimums. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer applied before the first winter can meaningfully cut water and salt absorption. Our <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">penetrating concrete sealer guide</a> covers the products made for that job, and <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">how to seal a concrete driveway</a> walks through the steps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pavers-sealed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Ice Melt Safe for Pavers, Stamped and Sealed Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete pavers handle moderate de-icer use better than poured slabs, while stamped and sealed surfaces need the gentlest products and the most care. In every case the joints and the sealer, not the paver itself, are usually what suffers first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do concrete pavers hold up?</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete pavers are made from dense, high-strength concrete and generally resist de-icers better than poured slabs, one reason they are popular in cold climates. Many paver manufacturers allow common de-icers but caution against excessive amounts and against products containing ammonium compounds. Rock salt or a blend used in moderation is usually acceptable on pavers, but check your manufacturer's guidance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What happens to the joint sand?</h3>

          <p>
            Joints are the weak point. Heavy brine and repeated shoveling can loosen and wash out joint sand, and some de-icers may affect polymeric sand binders. Shovel carefully along the joints, use a plastic blade where you can and inspect the joints in spring. Our <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">polymeric sand guide</a> covers which sands hold up best in freeze-thaw climates.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why do sealed and stamped surfaces need extra care?</h3>

          <p>
            Some de-icers can soften or whiten acrylic sealers on stamped, stained or paver surfaces, and metal shovel blades scrape them. A penetrating sealer generally tolerates winter conditions better than an acrylic film. On a sealed patio or a stamped driveway, gentler products like CMA or pet-safe melters are worth their higher price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which sealer suits a snowy climate?</h3>

          <p>
            Look at penetrating silane-siloxane products for areas that see snow and salt. Our <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">paver sealer guide</a> explains which chemistries hold up best, and the <a href="/best-water-based-paver-sealer-reviews/">water-based paver sealer roundup</a> includes penetrating options suited to cold climates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Much Ice Melt Should You Use, and When?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use only the label rate, typically a few ounces per square yard with granules spaced an inch or two apart, and put it down before or at the very start of a storm. Then clear the slush as soon as the ice breaks up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is less usually better?</h3>

          <p>
            Most people spread far more ice melt than they need. The label rate looks sparse, but more product does not melt proportionally faster; it mostly creates more brine to soak into the concrete. A handheld or walk-behind spreader lays down a far more even pattern than tossing handfuls, and it noticeably cuts the amount of product you use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is the best time to apply it?</h3>

          <p>
            Timing matters as much as quantity. A small amount spread before or right at the start of a storm stops ice from bonding to the surface, so the snow shovels off cleanly. This anti-icing approach uses less chemical than melting thick ice afterward. If the storm has already passed, shovel first and treat only the ice that remains.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you do once the ice breaks up?</h3>

          <p>
            Remove the slush, the single step that does the most to protect concrete. Brine and slush left on the surface soak in and refreeze overnight. Shovel or push it toward ground where it will not harm plants, ideally away from lawn edges and garden beds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you match the product to the temperature?</h3>

          <p>
            Check the forecast against the working range on the bag. Rock salt at 5 degrees Fahrenheit barely works, so it gets wasted and people pile on more. In mild winter weather, gentler products like CMA or pet-safe melters work fine; save the calcium chloride for deep-cold mornings on older concrete.
          </p>

