        <div id="liquid-granular" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Liquid or Granular Ice Melt Better for Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Liquid is easier on concrete for pre-treating, because it spreads evenly and uses less chemical; granular is better for breaking through ice that has already formed. Most homes get the best result from using both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do granules work best?</h3>

          <p>
            Pellets, crystals and flakes are what most homeowners use. They store easily, spread easily and work on existing ice by boring down through it. Their weakness is uneven coverage: granules land in clusters, bounce into lawns and form concentrated pockets of brine. Pellets also get tracked indoors and can scratch floors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do liquids work best?</h3>

          <p>
            Liquid de-icers are brines sprayed onto the surface, and their best use is anti-icing: sprayed on dry pavement before a storm, they leave a thin film that keeps snow and ice from bonding. They spread evenly, use far less chemical per square foot and start working right away. They are weaker against thick existing ice, and in very cold conditions the brine can refreeze if it is applied too heavily.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does control matter for concrete?</h3>

          <p>
            A measured, even film puts down less total chemical and avoids the pockets of concentrated brine that granules leave, which for many homeowners matters more than the chemistry. The catch is that most liquid de-icers are chloride brines, so the same rules about new and decorative concrete still apply.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you combine the two?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes, and it works well: liquid pre-treatment before forecast storms, prompt shoveling, and a gentle granular product to spot-treat whatever ice remains. Dedicate a pump sprayer to the liquid de-icer and rinse it after use, since salt brine corrodes metal parts, and never share that sprayer with sealers or garden chemicals.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pets-plants" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Ice Melt Is Safe for Pets, Plants and Floors?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Salt-free or pet-focused melters are the safest choice around pets, keeping chloride slush off beds protects plants, and good mats protect indoor floors. No de-icer, however it is marketed, is safe to eat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do chloride salts affect pets?</h3>

          <p>
            They are hard on paws, drying and cracking the pads, and pets that lick their feet afterward can get an upset stomach, or worse after large amounts. Calcium chloride is the harshest because it gives off heat as it dissolves. If pets use a walk, choose a pet-focused product and wipe paws when they come inside. Booties help with dogs that tolerate them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you protect lawns and garden beds?</h3>

          <p>
            Chlorides harm plants too. Salty slush thrown onto lawns and beds damages roots and soil structure, and the damage often appears in spring as browned grass edges and struggling shrubs. Pile snow away from beds where possible, use gentler products along garden edges and water the soil deeply in spring to flush salt below the root zone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about indoor floors?</h3>

          <p>
            De-icer residue leaves white films on hardwood, tile and carpet, and grit scratches finishes. Calcium and magnesium chloride residues can be oily and hard to clean. A coarse outdoor mat and an absorbent indoor mat at every entry catch most of it; clean whatever gets through promptly with a damp mop and a cleaner made for salt residue.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does pet-safe mean edible?</h3>

          <p>
            No. Even products marketed as safe for pets should not be eaten in quantity, and the label usually says so. Store bags out of reach, sweep up leftover pellets once the ice is gone, and call your veterinarian if a pet eats a large amount of any de-icer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Much Does Ice Melt Cost Per Winter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A moderate winter on a typical driveway, walk and steps might use 50 to 150 pounds of a chloride product, and gentle specialty melters used at similar rates cost several times as much over the season. Spending where it matters keeps the total reasonable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is price per bag misleading?</h3>

          <p>
            The products are used at very different rates. Rock salt and mined chloride products are the cheapest per pound, often well under a dollar a pound in 50-pound bags at the time of writing. Calcium chloride pellets cost more per pound but go down more sparingly. Pet-safe and acetate products commonly run several dollars a pound, and pure CMA is usually the most expensive option per pound.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you estimate a season?</h3>

          <p>
            Measure only the areas you actually treat. A typical two-car driveway, front walk and steps add up to roughly 800 to 1,000 square feet. At label rates, a moderate winter with a dozen icing events might use 50 to 150 pounds of a chloride product: less if you pre-treat and shovel diligently, more if you broadcast heavily.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does a split strategy save money?</h3>

          <p>
            Spend on the gentle, expensive product only where it matters most: the front steps, the walk the dog uses, stamped or stained patios and any concrete less than a couple of years old. Use a moderately priced blend on the older driveway, and keep a small bag of calcium chloride for the handful of deep-cold mornings each year. That protects the vulnerable surfaces without paying premium prices for every square foot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does damage cost by comparison?</h3>

          <p>
            Replacing a scaled concrete walk or resurfacing a driveway costs thousands of dollars, far more than a decade of premium ice melt. A few extra dollars a season for gentler products on vulnerable surfaces, plus a penetrating sealer every several years, is cheap insurance for concrete you expect to last thirty years or more.
          </p>

