        <div id="protect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. How Do You Protect Concrete From Ice Melt Damage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Seal it with a penetrating sealer, fix the drainage, use plastic shovel blades and rinse the slab in spring. Together those habits do more for a slab than any single product choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why use a penetrating sealer?</h3>

          <p>
            Silane and siloxane sealers soak into concrete and make it shed water and brine, which reduces both freeze-thaw scaling and chloride absorption. Apply in fall, on dry concrete, within the temperature range on the label, typically every several years. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> compares the main options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does drainage affect scaling?</h3>

          <p>
            Scaling starts in the low spots where melt pools. Point downspouts away from walks and driveways, and keep snow piles from draining across the slab. Patches of standing water that freeze overnight do more damage than the de-icer itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the shovel matter?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. Use plastic blades on decorative and sealed surfaces, and set snowblower skids so the auger does not scrape the slab. Metal edges gouge sealers and chip the surface of weakened concrete, opening more pathways for water to get in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why rinse in spring?</h3>

          <p>
            Once temperatures stay above freezing, hose off driveways, walks and patios to remove leftover salt residue, and flush the garden beds beside them. A quick spring rinse clears the chloride that would otherwise keep working into the surface through the next season.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE WINTER RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Shovel more, melt less, and never let brine sit overnight.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The product matters, but how you use it matters more. A gentle melt piled on and left as slush can do more harm than a modest amount of rock salt spread thinly and cleared promptly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes With Ice Melt on Concrete
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Salting a brand-new slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete poured within the past year is the most prone to scaling, and even melters advertised as concrete safe often warn against new concrete in the fine print. Through that first winter, shovel promptly, use sand or grit for traction, and rinse away any street brine that cars drip onto the slab.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Piling on extra product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Doubling the dose does not double the melting; it mainly makes more brine to soak into the slab and refreeze. Use a spreader, stick to the label rate and expect the granules to look widely spaced. Pre-treating before a storm takes far less product than chasing thick ice afterward.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the wrong melt for the temperature</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rock salt slows sharply below about 15 degrees Fahrenheit, so people keep pouring and see little result, and that subzero pile simply becomes more brine later. Check the forecast against the working range on the bag, save stronger melt for the coldest days and use gentler products in mild weather.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving slush on the slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Slush left sitting on concrete soaks in and refreezes overnight, which is exactly what drives scaling. As soon as the ice breaks up, shovel or push the slush off, toward a spot where it will not drain back across the concrete. This one habit protects a slab more than switching brands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Grabbing fertilizer as a de-icer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ammonium nitrate and ammonium sulfate fertilizers will melt ice, but ammonium compounds attack concrete chemically and can cause rapid deterioration. If you run out of melt, spread sand, grit or non-clumping cat litter for traction until you can buy a proper product, and keep a spare bag of gentle melt on hand before winter.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

