        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which de-icer is the gentlest on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Calcium magnesium acetate, sold as CMA, is generally regarded as the gentlest common de-icer for concrete and steel, and pet-safe products based on urea or amides are also low risk. Both melt more slowly and work less well in deep cold than chlorides. No de-icer is entirely harmless, since any meltwater can soak in and refreeze, so prompt shoveling still matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is rock salt or calcium chloride worse for concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Calcium chloride works in far colder weather, but research ties it, together with magnesium chloride, to chemical damage in cement paste on top of freeze-thaw scaling. Rock salt mainly drives scaling and corrodes rebar. On older, sound concrete, either is acceptable in small amounts. On new or decorative concrete, skip both and use CMA, a pet-safe melter or sand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should new concrete go without de-icers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Common guidance says to keep de-icing chemicals off concrete through its first winter, and some contractors recommend waiting two winters after a late-season pour. In the meantime, rely on shoveling and sand for traction. Once the concrete has cured, typically at least 28 days, a penetrating sealer adds protection for the winters that follow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will de-icers hurt my pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Concrete pavers are dense and generally handle moderate use of common de-icers better than poured slabs, though many paver makers caution against heavy use and ammonium-based products. Joint sand is the bigger concern, since brine and shoveling can loosen it. Clay pavers and natural stone vary widely, so follow the manufacturer's guidance and favor gentler products on sealed surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How cold is too cold for ice melt to work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That depends on the chemistry. Rock salt slows sharply below about 15 to 20 degrees Fahrenheit, and CMA and urea products work best above roughly 20 degrees. Magnesium chloride keeps working to about 0 to 5 degrees, while calcium chloride works well below zero. Read the working range on the bag and match the product to the forecast.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are liquid de-icers easier on concrete than pellets?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can be, because liquids spread evenly and use less total chemical, especially when sprayed before a storm to keep ice from bonding. They are weaker at cutting through thick ice that has already formed. Most are chloride brines, so keep them off new or decorative concrete, and avoid over-applying them on any slab.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal concrete to protect it from salt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most exterior concrete in cold climates, yes. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer limits how much water and brine soak into the slab, which slows scaling and chloride damage. Apply it in fall to dry, cured concrete within the temperature range on the label, and reapply every several years as the manufacturer directs.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

